THOUGHT: The error is that longAdapter.read(in) may return null. I should mirror the pattern used in the AtomicLong adapter above: store the result in a local variable, check for null, and decide on a safe behavior. Since this is an internal helper and the list is of primitive longs, the safest is to throw a JsonSyntaxException if a null is encountered, avoiding silent defaulting. I’ll edit Gson.java accordingly and then run the compile task to confirm that the specific NullAway error is gone and no new ones are introduced.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/gson && sed -i '452,458s/AtomicLongArray read(JsonReader in) throws IOException {/AtomicLongArray read(JsonReader in) throws IOException {/' gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/Gson.java && sed -i '455,458c\        while (in.hasNext()) {
          Number number = longAdapter.read(in);
          if (number == null) {
            throw new com.google.gson.JsonSyntaxException("null value for long in array");
          }
          long value = number.longValue();
          list.add(value);
        }' gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/Gson.java && ./gradlew :gson:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && tail -n 60 new_build_output.log
```

